      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-2$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-2)^{′}=4{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{4}+{x}_{n}^{3}-2}{4{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2})-({x}_{n}^{4}+{x}_{n}^{3}-2)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}+2{x}_{n}^{3}+2}{4{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{4}+2{x}_{0}^{3}+2}{4{x}_{0}^{3}+3{x}_{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(0.5)^{4}+2(0.5)^{3}+2}{4(0.5)^{3}+3(0.5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.4375}{1.25} \\\\  & =1.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
